const chapterBriefs=[["Start a field-trial project", "A researcher has harvest records from six plots. How can a saved R script turn those observations into a result another student can reproduce?", "No previous programming is assumed. Distinguish an instruction from its printed result.", "Create a yield vector, calculate its mean, change one observation, and rerun the script. Explain why the result changes.", "Typing commands into the console without saving the script loses the record of how the answer was obtained."], ["Represent a farm correctly", "A farm register contains plot identifiers, crop names, yields and missing measurements. Which R structures preserve their meaning?", "Be able to assign an object and call a function.", "Build a small data frame, inspect each column with str(), and select a single observation. Explain why treatment labels should not be treated as numeric measurements.", "A numeric treatment code is a label unless its numerical distance has a scientific meaning. R indexing begins at 1."], ["Bring observations into R", "An Excel workbook and a CSV file describe the same trial. How would you check that both imports represent the same observations?", "Understand data frames, column names and working directories.", "Import a file, inspect dimensions and types, check units and missing-value codes, and export a clean copy without row numbers.", "A successful import does not prove that dates, decimal separators, units or missing-value codes were interpreted correctly."], ["Clean without rewriting the evidence", "A soil dataset contains repeated rows, a missing nitrogen value and one unusually high reading. What should be corrected, retained or investigated?", "Know how to inspect a data frame and select rows.", "Count missing values and duplicates before making changes. Compare summaries before and after a documented cleaning decision.", "An extreme value is not automatically an error. Deleting it merely because it changes the answer is not a defensible cleaning rule."], ["Compare like with like", "Several farms received different treatments. Which grouping variables are needed to summarize their yields fairly?", "Understand missing values and the difference between rows and columns.", "Filter a defined population, group by treatment and summarize n, mean and SD. Confirm the result with a second method.", "A mean without its sample size can conceal missing records or unequal group sizes. Grouping does not create independent replication."], ["Choose a plot that answers a question", "You need to explain variation in yield and its relationship with rainfall. Which graph would answer each question?", "Distinguish categorical and continuous variables.", "Draw a distribution plot and a scatter plot. Change labels and units, compare treatment groups, and explain a visible pattern without claiming causation.", "Bar charts of means can hide the distribution. A striking pattern can arise from an outlier or a confounding variable."], ["Describe a variable, not just its average", "Two fields have similar mean yield but different variability. Which summaries reveal the practical difference?", "Be able to create vectors and recognize missing observations.", "Calculate mean, median, SD and quartiles. Change one extreme observation and compare the sensitivity of mean and median.", "SD describes spread among observations; SE describes uncertainty in an estimated mean. They answer different questions."], ["Investigate before fitting a model", "A crop-yield dataset includes rainfall, fertilizer and farm identifiers. What must be understood before selecting a model?", "Use summaries, grouping and exploratory plots.", "Audit the observations, inspect distributions and relationships, identify plausible confounding, and write an analysis question before modelling.", "Exploratory associations generate questions. They do not establish that changing one factor will cause yield to change."], ["Explain a crop growth curve", "A crop accumulates biomass rapidly and then levels off. What do the asymptote, growth rate and inflection time mean biologically?", "Understand a function, a scatter plot and a fitted model.", "Compare logistic and Gompertz curves. Change one parameter while holding others fixed, then assess residuals and biological plausibility.", "A visually close curve can still have implausible parameters. Extrapolation beyond the observed time range needs particular care."], ["Respect the order of time", "Monthly rainfall has a seasonal cycle. How should you assess forecasts that must predict observations not yet seen?", "Understand plotting, missing values and regular observation intervals.", "Construct a monthly series, distinguish trend from seasonality, compare a simple baseline with a fitted forecast, and evaluate on later held-out observations.", "Randomly splitting a time series can leak future information. A good in-sample fit does not establish forecasting performance."], ["Match the test to the design", "A pest-control trial records measurements before and after treatment on the same plots. What makes this comparison paired?", "Understand variation, independent observations and exploratory checks.", "State the null hypothesis, identify the experimental unit, choose a design-appropriate test, and interpret its estimate, interval and p-value.", "A p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true. Statistical significance alone does not describe practical importance."], ["Keep the field design in the analysis", "Treatments were randomized within field blocks. How can the analysis account for the variation the blocking was intended to control?", "Understand hypotheses, factors and independent experimental units.", "Identify the design, fit a model containing the appropriate treatment and block terms, inspect diagnostics, and interpret comparisons in context.", "Subsamples within a plot do not create additional treatment replicates. Ignoring blocks can change the error term and inference."], ["Separate prediction from explanation", "Yield rises with rainfall in a sample of farms. How would you describe the association and assess predictions for another farm?", "Know scatter plots, model fitting and residual variation.", "Fit a regression, interpret coefficients with units, inspect residuals, and compare interval predictions within and outside the observed range.", "Correlation does not establish causation. A coefficient from a multiple regression is conditional on the other variables in that model."], ["Produce an analysis someone can audit", "Another student must reproduce your report from its source and data. What must the project contain, including when AI helped write the code?", "Be able to save a script, explain an analysis and inspect its output.", "Create a Quarto report with computed results, render it in a clean session, and document any substantive AI assistance and verification.", "A report can render successfully while using an unsuitable analysis. You remain responsible for the code, assumptions, data and conclusions."]];
